// solutions/ontological/lib/services/conventions/ops/show.ts
init_zod();
init_registry();
var CONVENTIONS = {
  assets: `assets — plugin-level files, copied verbatim

A plugin sometimes ships more than prose skills: a built CLI, a wasm
artifact, a launcher script — files authored on disk, not as vault notes.
Declare them under the project file's top-level \`assets:\` key:

    assets:
      - from: cli/sdlc      # resolved against agent-pants.yaml's own directory
        to: cli/sdlc        # relative to the built plugin's own root
        # plugin: sdlc      # optional; defaults to marketplace.default_plugin

\`agent-pants build\`/\`check\` copy each entry's bytes (and its executable
bit) into \`<packs>/<plugin>/<to>\` unmodified — never compiled, never
link-resolved — and \`check\` reports drift the same way it does for a
rendered skill, if the source changes on disk. A \`from:\` naming a
directory lands its whole tree under \`<to>\`, minus any \`tests\`/\`fixtures\`
directory (see \`sdlc conventions show excludes\`) and any \`*.test.*\` file. Naming a plugin the vault
holds no skill, command or hook for is refused as \`unknown-asset-plugin\`
rather than silently dropped.

The sdlc plugin you installed was built this way: its \`cli/sdlc\` launcher
(what every \`\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/cli/sdlc\` shell-out and every
\`scripts/sdlc\` shim — see \`sdlc conventions show shim\` — runs) and the
reference docs its skills read through \`\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/…\` are
assets, not vault notes.`,
  excludes: `excludes — tests/ and fixtures/ never ship

A skill's bundle root ships every file under it verbatim, resource for
resource — but a directory literally named \`tests\` or \`fixtures\`, at any
depth under that root, never does. A directory \`assets:\` entry follows the
same rule, and also drops every \`*.test.*\` file. This is a fixed agent-pants convention,
not a project-file setting: nothing to declare, nothing to forget to
declare, and it applies to every skill in every build.

This exists because a skill's own dev-only fixtures can look like real
Obsidian syntax to the build's link-resolution pass. sdlc's own
\`entities-audit\` skill ships \`tests/fixtures/**/*.md\` carrying literal
\`[[D-9999-some-decision]]\`-shaped strings as test data; without the
exclude, the build's bundle-resource sweep reads that as a broken wikilink
and refuses the whole build with \`unresolved-syntax\` — on a vault that has
nothing actually wrong with it. The exclude keeps those directories out of
the sweep entirely, so they are neither validated as links nor shipped in
the built plugin.

If a skill genuinely needs to ship a directory that would collide with this
name, rename it — there is no per-skill override, by design: the whole
point is that no skill has to remember to ask for it.`,
  shim: `shim — the per-skill scripts/sdlc fallback

Every skill that ships alongside a \`cli/sdlc\` asset (see
\`sdlc conventions show assets\`) also gets a generated \`scripts/sdlc\` file
in its own built directory: a small POSIX \`sh\` script that walks up from
its own location looking for a sibling \`cli/sdlc\`, and \`exec\`s it with
every argument forwarded. Reaching the top of the tree with nothing found
falls back to bare \`sdlc\` on \`PATH\`.

Most skills should reach the CLI more directly, as
\`\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/cli/sdlc\` — Claude renders that token at
invocation time, which is the shorter route and needs no shim at all. The
shim exists for the call sites that have no such token to render: a hook
script, a test harness driving a skill's shell-outs directly, or another
harness's runtime that lays out its plugin tree differently.

The shim is generated, not authored — it carries a "GENERATED by
agent-pants; do not edit" header and is identical across every skill it
appears in. A plugin that declares no \`cli/sdlc\` asset gets no shim at
all: the shim's only job is finding an asset that plugin never ships.`
};
